<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Ellipi</h1><p class="st-meta">850 BCE – 690 BCE (approx.) · capital Marubishtu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the central Zagros, an Assyrian ally and vassal until a disputed succession drew in Sargon II and Sennacherib.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ellipi lay in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> and northern Luristan region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, between Media to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> to the south. It appears in Assyrian sources from the 9th century BCE as a kingdom with fortified towns, its royal seat at Marubishtu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ellipi's kings controlled valuable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse-breeding</a> lands and routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to the plateau. Its best-known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalta">Dalta</a>, was a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>. When Dalta died in 708 BCE, his sons Nibe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishpabara">Ishpabara</a> fought for the throne; Nibe called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and Ishpabara on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, which sent troops to install him. Some scholars associate the region with the Luristan bronzes, though this is debated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishpabara">Ishpabara</a> later turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, and in 702 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> sacked Marubishtu and annexed part of Ellipi. The rest of the kingdom fades from the record by the early 7th century BCE, absorbed into the Median sphere.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ellipi.jpg" alt="Ellipi" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Ali957 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_5_ellipi_provinces.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 BCE – 690 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marubishtu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dalta, Ishpabara, Nibe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Ellipi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-770&amp;focus=ellipi" aria-label="Ellipi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6099 1015 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ellipi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6231 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6419 1271h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6419 1271h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=-770&amp;focus=ellipi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
